"""
Power Analysis: Sample Size for Two-Proportion Z-Test — how many samples do I need?

WHAT IT DOES: Before running an A/B experiment, this computes the minimum sample size
per group needed to reliably detect a difference between two proportions (e.g.
conversion 30% vs 50%). Too few samples → real effects go unnoticed; too many →
wasted time/money. Uses normal approximation with a self-check: the computed n is
verified to achieve the target power, and n−1 is proven to give strictly less power.

WHEN TO USE: You're designing an experiment and need to know the sample size before
you start collecting data. The answer is always "it depends" — this function tells
you exactly how much.

GUARANTEES: The computed n achieves at least (target_power − 0.05) power. n−1 gives
strictly lower power (the computed n is sufficient and not padded). The power
computation uses a two-tailed z-test with pooled standard error under H0.

NOT: A general-purpose power calculator. Only handles two-proportion z-tests (one-sided
alternative). Does not handle continuous outcomes, t-tests, ANOVA, or survival analysis.
"""
